{:edb/db-before-t 1325 :edb/db-after-t 1326 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534638 50 #inst "2026-10-12T05:32:45.106Z" 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186128976 1071 "Compute the eigenvalues of the matrix ![](images/11/1198325c2b7aeab6d1c4e5f405f1e979d408c4c8f128851f039202d4fa94c166.svg)" 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186128976 1071 "Compute the eigenvalues of the matrix ![](images/61/61d1e4670c482f79d4b87ff36bb772339a704df18ccfb624ce183729f5022c4c.png)" 13194139534638 17592186045417 false] [17592186128976 1074 "First, we find the matrix $\\, A-\\lambda I \\mathbin{:}$\n\n﻿﻿﻿![](images/8b/8bfd617b360f6bed1fcf835ebe52c56f3c7b6a5a5234e099f7a0aec0d4bbdc36.png)\n\nThen, we compute the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-2(-2) & =0 \\\\ 1-2\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(1)(5)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{-16}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm 4\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda=1\\pm 2\\textrm{i}.$" 13194139534638 17592186045417 false] [17592186128976 1074 "First, we find the matrix $\\, A-\\lambda I \\mathbin{:}$\n\n﻿﻿﻿![](images/97/97e30eb12530173f3d4b7f90aa3dbbf9cef187ef33154f4da600a393c5777b43.svg)\n\nThen, we compute the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-2(-2) & =0 \\\\ 1-2\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-2)\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(1)(5)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{-16}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm 4\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda=1\\pm 2\\textrm{i}.$" 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186129159 1038 #edb/ref 17592186430455 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186129359 1050 420.0 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186130351 1064 "Let's compute the eigenvalues of the following matrix:\n\n![](images/1b/1bc3c21f31a351f7f7281824a0d6db9fce0c1581f6506e6533c2352fe6460788.svg)\n\nThe characteristic equation of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 +1 = 0.\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues are the roots of the characteristic equation. However, the discriminant of this equation is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D}=-4<0,\n$$\n\nso $A$ doesn't have any real eigenvalues! \n\nNonetheless, we can still compute the eigenvalues of $A.$ The difference here is that they will be complex numbers. Let's solve the characteristic equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+1=0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}=-1 \\\\ \\lambda =\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda = \\pm\\textrm{i}.$" 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186130351 1064 "Let's compute the eigenvalues of the following matrix:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 +1 = 0.\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues are the roots of the characteristic equation. However, the discriminant of this equation is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D}=-4<0,\n$$\n\nso $A$ doesn't have any real eigenvalues! \n\nNonetheless, we can still compute the eigenvalues of $A.$ The difference here is that they will be complex numbers. Let's solve the characteristic equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+1=0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}=-1 \\\\ \\lambda =\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda = \\pm\\textrm{i}.$" 13194139534638 17592186045417 false] [17592186130352 1064 "You may have noticed that if $\\lambda$ is a complex eigenvalue of a real matrix $A,$ then the complex conjugate $\\overline{\\lambda}$ also appears to be an eigenvalue.\n\nMoreover, if the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ corresponds to an eigenvector $\\mathbb{v},$ then the eigenvalue $\\overline{\\lambda}$ corresponds to an eigenvector $\\overline{\\mathbb v}.$\n\nAs a matter of fact, this is true for all matrices with real entries:\n\n> Let $A$ be a matrix with real entries. If $A$ has an eigenvector $\\mathbb v$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda,$ then $A$ also has an eigenvector $\\overline{\\mathbb v}$ with eigenvalue $\\overline{\\lambda}.$\n\nLet's prove this. If a real matrix $A$ has an eigenvalue $\\lambda$ with corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf{v},$ then\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{v}\t=\t\\lambda\\mathbf{v}.\n$$\n\nTaking the complex conjugate of both sides, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{Av} & =\\overset{―}{\\lambda v} \\\\ \\overset{―}{A}\\overset{―}{v} & =\\overset{―}{\\lambda }\\overset{―}{v}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut since $A$ is a matrix with real entries, we have that $\\overline{A} = A.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA\\overline{\\mathbf{v}} = \\overline{\\lambda}\\overline{\\mathbf{v}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\overline{\\lambda}$ is also an eigenvalue of $A$ and its corresponding eigenvector is $\\overline{\\mathbf{v}}.$" 13194139534638 17592186045417 false] [17592186130352 1064 "You may have noticed that if $\\lambda$ is a complex eigenvalue of a real matrix $A,$ then the complex conjugate $\\overline{\\lambda}$ also appears to be an eigenvalue.\n\nMoreover, if the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ corresponds to an eigenvector $\\mathbb{v},$ then the eigenvalue $\\overline{\\lambda}$ corresponds to an eigenvector $\\overline{\\mathbb v}.$\n\nAs a matter of fact, this is true for all matrices with real entries:\n\nLet $A$ be a matrix with real entries. If $A$ has an eigenvector $\\mathbb v$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda,$ then $A$ also has an eigenvector $\\overline{\\mathbb v}$ with eigenvalue $\\overline{\\lambda}.$\n\nLet's prove this. If a real matrix $A$ has an eigenvalue $\\lambda$ with corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf{v},$ then\n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{v}\t=\t\\lambda\\mathbf{v}.\n$$\n\nTaking the complex conjugate of both sides, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{Av} & =\\overset{―}{\\lambda v} \\\\ \\overset{―}{A}\\overset{―}{v} & =\\overset{―}{\\lambda }\\overset{―}{v}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut since $A$ is a matrix with real entries, we have that $\\overline{A} = A.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nA\\overline{\\mathbf{v}} = \\overline{\\lambda}\\overline{\\mathbf{v}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\overline{\\lambda}$ is also an eigenvalue of $A$ and its corresponding eigenvector is $\\overline{\\mathbf{v}}.$" 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186430449 1080 "\\frac{3}{2}\\text{i}" 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186430449 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186430449 1083 #uuid "1b833791-e910-521c-87da-41147c8cc0ef" 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186430450 1080 "3\\text{i}" 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186430450 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186430450 1083 #uuid "20b4f533-7424-5726-bf17-08f22b518fc4" 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186430451 1080 "2" 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186430451 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186430451 1083 #uuid "9f63ef42-06cc-58ad-b830-813ec429fb50" 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186430452 1080 "-2" 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186430452 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186430452 1083 #uuid "d29b5d3e-3568-573d-86cb-207f80fa38a7" 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186430453 1080 "0" 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186430453 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186430453 1083 #uuid "dba03e7d-2a34-55c7-a14a-96455d27dcd9" 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186430454 1075 #edb/ref 17592186430449 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186430454 1075 #edb/ref 17592186430450 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186430454 1075 #edb/ref 17592186430451 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186430454 1075 #edb/ref 17592186430452 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186430454 1075 #edb/ref 17592186430453 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186430454 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186430454 1077 "selection" 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186430454 1078 #edb/ref 17592186430451 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186430454 1079 #uuid "5e4f4638-8952-5dfa-8fd2-e5c0fe083b3a" 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186430455 1068 #edb/ref 17592186430454 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186430455 1069 "q-81181" 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186430455 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186430455 1071 "Given that ${\\lambda }_{1}=4+3\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}$ are eigenvalues of a real $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$, find $\\frac{1}{4}({\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}).$" 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186430455 1072 #uuid "408fe19d-d084-5aed-90d7-346f39925c23" 13194139534638 17592186045417 true] [17592186430455 1074 "Since ${\\lambda }_{1}=4+3\\text{i}$ is a complex eigenvalue of $A,$ the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of ${\\lambda }_{1}.$\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}=4-3\\text{i}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}({\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2}) & =\\frac{1}{4}((4+3\\text{i})+(4-3\\text{i})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(8) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534638 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-1b833791-e910-521c-87da-41147c8cc0ef" 175